  "query_preview": "..\n\nToday: With the current system exiting the area early this \nmorning, cooler air will move in behind it. With soil \ntemperatures dipping into the low 30s/upper 20s this morning, \ntemperature may be warmer than the environment, which has a low \nchance (25%) of leading to patchy fog. Gusty winds this morning \nwill reduce as the wind gradient loosen up through the day \nThursday. \n\nTonight through Tuesday: Large scale upper troughing continues \nacross the western US the remainder of the week. The next system \nwill bring another shot of arctic air from the north this weekend.\nSome light snow is possible but the main impact will be the below\nnormal temps. Wind chills this weekend are forecast to be in the \nteens during the AM hours and potentially dipping in the single \ndigits. The frontal passage Friday night/Saturday morning may \nbring speeds back up high enough to warrant a Lake Wind Advisory \nthat morning. Will keep an eye on this time period. \n\nTuesday night onward: A weak ridge to zonal flow pattern will set\nin leading the region back to warmer near normal temps with highs\nin the 30s and 40s. In general this should also reduce chance for\nprecip from here onward.\n\n\nFORECAST CONFIDENCE & DEVIATIONS:\n- High confidence through Friday for most of the forecast.\n\n- Confidence in fog developing this morning is low (10%-20%).\n\n- Moderate confidence Friday night through Monday night.\n\n- Low confidence Tuesday onward.\n\nFINDLEY/GAH",
